        "response": "The hepatic-related exclusion criterion (Criterion 11) in the TJ301 clinical trial is as follows:\n\n**Exclusion Criterion 11 \u2013 Known Clinically Relevant Chronic Liver Disease:**\n\nThere are two scenarios described:\n\n**A) In the absence of a diagnosis of Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis (PSC):**\nA subject is excluded if they have impaired hepatic function defined by any of the following:\n- Serum transaminases **>2.5 \u00d7 ULN** (Upper Limit of Normal)\n- Alkaline phosphatase **>2.5 \u00d7 ULN**\n- Abnormalities in synthetic liver function tests judged by the investigator to be **clinically significant**\n\n(Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11; p. 35)\n\n**B) With a diagnosis of Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis (PSC):**\nA subject is excluded if they have:\n- Serum transaminases **>3 \u00d7 ULN**\n- Alkaline phosphatase **>3 \u00d7 ULN**\n- Abnormalities in synthetic liver function tests, specifically **total bilirubin >1.5 \u00d7 ULN**, judged by the investigator to be **clinically significant**\n\n(Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11; p. 35)\n\n> In summary, PSC patients are held to a **slightly higher threshold** for transaminases and alkaline phosphatase (3\u00d7 vs. 2.5\u00d7 ULN), acknowledging that PSC itself may cause some baseline elevation in these markers.",
